at you had a lot of  confidence in her opinion in the first  place  if ever there was a false confession  case  i could not imagine how it would be  supported more  than in a case like this  okay so let's jump in for some analysis  right now terry what's your assessment  of the defendant's  choice to be tried by a judge rather  than a jury here  the jury was hung the last time and as  criminal defense attorneys like brian  buckmeyer might often say  all you need is that one juror to shut  down the state's case  if they tried this in front of a jury  they could have another hung jury the  state may have to give up  so why go with the judge i couldn't  agree with you more aaron i mean most  criminal defendants they're going to  take advantage of the sixth amendment  right to a jury of your peers  and here for whatever reason she's  deciding to put her fate before the  judge  and we know judges are going to apply  the law and they're going to be  objective so  i think it's the wrong choice  particularly since she did fairly well  in front of the jury before yeah brian  how would  would you advise this client you know  would you say this is a good idea to go  with a bench trial this time around  i mean me personally no i i for example  trust a juror of peers more than i would  a single judge i actually put in an  order it's  jury judge bears and then prosecutors in  terms of who i trust more  uh and i wouldn't do this but if i get a  wink and a nod from a judge instead of  technical argument  which i think it might be then maybe  just maybe  you go with a bench trial yeah there may  be some uh you know one of those little  wink and nods here where  maybe the defense thinks that the judge  might be a little bit favorable to a  technical legal argument here i agree  that that might happen  so brian how would you defend kelsey  thomas factually would you turn around  and say the confession was coerced she  didn't do it  this wouldn't be a factual argument  again because it